@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Numeric Functions";
}

<div class="container-fluid py-4">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold">
            SQL Server Numeric Functions
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            Numeric Functions in SQL Server are used to perform
            mathematical calculations and operations on numeric data.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INTRODUCTION
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What are Numeric Functions?
        </h2>

        <p>
            Numeric functions are built-in SQL Server functions that
            are used to perform calculations and manipulate numerical
            values.
        </p>

        <p>
            They are commonly used for rounding numbers, finding
            absolute values, calculating powers, square roots and
            performing other mathematical operations.
        </p>

        <div class="alert alert-info mt-4">

            <strong>Example:</strong>

            <p class="mb-0 mt-2">
                If a database contains product prices such as
                <strong>125.75</strong>, <strong>99.49</strong> and
                <strong>-50</strong>, numeric functions can be used
                to round values, find absolute values and perform
                mathematical calculations.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         COMMON NUMERIC FUNCTIONS
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Common Numeric Functions
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped align-middle">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Function</th>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                        <th>Example</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>ABS()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Returns the absolute value of a number.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>ABS(-25)</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>CEILING()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Returns the smallest integer greater than
                            or equal to the specified number.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>CEILING(12.4)</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>FLOOR()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Returns the largest integer less than
                            or equal to the specified number.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>FLOOR(12.8)</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>ROUND()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Rounds a number to the specified number
                            of decimal places.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>ROUND(125.678, 2)</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>POWER()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Returns a number raised to a specified power.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>POWER(2, 3)</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>SQRT()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Returns the square root of a number.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>SQRT(25)</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>SIGN()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Returns the sign of a number.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>SIGN(-10)</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>RAND()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Returns a pseudo-random floating-point
                            value between 0 and 1.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>RAND()</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         1. ABS()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            1. ABS() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>ABS()</strong> function returns the absolute
            value of a number.
        </p>

        <p>
            An absolute value is always positive or zero.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>ABS(number)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT ABS(-25);</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> 25
        </div>

        <p>
            The negative value <strong>-25</strong> becomes
            <strong>25</strong>.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         2. CEILING()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            2. CEILING() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>CEILING()</strong> function returns the
            smallest integer that is greater than or equal to
            the specified number.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>CEILING(number)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT CEILING(12.4);</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> 13
        </div>

        <p>
            13 is the smallest integer greater than or equal to 12.4.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         3. FLOOR()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            3. FLOOR() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>FLOOR()</strong> function returns the largest
            integer that is less than or equal to the specified number.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>FLOOR(number)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT FLOOR(12.8);</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> 12
        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         4. ROUND()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            4. ROUND() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>ROUND()</strong> function rounds a numeric
            value to a specified number of decimal places.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>ROUND(number, decimal_places)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT ROUND(125.678, 2);</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> 125.68
        </div>

        <p>
            The number is rounded to two decimal places.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         5. POWER()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            5. POWER() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>POWER()</strong> function returns a number
            raised to the specified power.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>POWER(number, power)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT POWER(2, 3);</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> 8
        </div>

        <p>
            Here, 2 is raised to the power of 3:
            <strong>2 × 2 × 2 = 8</strong>.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         6. SQRT()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            6. SQRT() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>SQRT()</strong> function returns the square
            root of a specified number.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SQRT(number)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT SQRT(25);</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> 5
        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         7. SIGN()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            7. SIGN() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>SIGN()</strong> function returns the sign
            of a numeric expression.
        </p>

        <ul>

            <li>
                <strong>-1</strong> is returned for a negative number.
            </li>

            <li>
                <strong>0</strong> is returned for zero.
            </li>

            <li>
                <strong>1</strong> is returned for a positive number.
            </li>

        </ul>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SIGN(number)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT SIGN(-10);</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> -1
        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         8. RAND()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            8. RAND() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>RAND()</strong> function returns a pseudo-random
            floating-point value between 0 and 1.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>RAND()</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT RAND();</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> A random decimal value between 0 and 1.
        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         PRACTICAL TABLE EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Practical Example with a Table
        </h2>

        <p>
            Suppose we have a Products table containing product prices.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE TABLE Products
(
    ProductId INT,
    ProductName VARCHAR(100),
    Price DECIMAL(10,2)
);</code></pre>


        <p class="mt-4">
            Insert sample data:
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>INSERT INTO Products
VALUES
(1, 'Keyboard', 125.75),
(2, 'Mouse', 99.49),
(3, 'Monitor', 450.80);</code></pre>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Round Product Prices
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT
    ProductName,
    Price,
    ROUND(Price, 0) AS RoundedPrice
FROM Products;</code></pre>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Find Absolute Value
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT
    ABS(-500) AS AbsoluteValue;</code></pre>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Round Price Up
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT
    ProductName,
    Price,
    CEILING(Price) AS CeilingPrice
FROM Products;</code></pre>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Round Price Down
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT
    ProductName,
    Price,
    FLOOR(Price) AS FloorPrice
FROM Products;</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         CEILING VS FLOOR VS ROUND
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            CEILING() vs FLOOR() vs ROUND()
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Function</th>
                        <th>Example</th>
                        <th>Result</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>CEILING()</td>
                        <td><code>CEILING(12.4)</code></td>
                        <td>13</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>FLOOR()</td>
                        <td><code>FLOOR(12.8)</code></td>
                        <td>12</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>ROUND()</td>
                        <td><code>ROUND(12.56, 1)</code></td>
                        <td>12.6</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         WHEN TO USE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            When Do We Use Numeric Functions?
        </h2>

        <ul>

            <li class="mb-2">
                When mathematical calculations are required.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                When decimal values need to be rounded.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                When positive values are required from negative numbers.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                When calculating powers or square roots.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                When values need to be rounded up or down.
            </li>

            <li>
                When processing numerical data stored in database tables.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-warning">

            <h4 class="fw-bold">
                Important Points
            </h4>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    ABS() returns the absolute value.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    CEILING() rounds a value upward to an integer.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    FLOOR() rounds a value downward to an integer.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    ROUND() rounds a value to the specified decimal places.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    POWER() is used for exponent calculations.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    SQRT() returns the square root.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    SIGN() identifies whether a number is negative,
                    zero or positive.
                </li>

                <li>
                    RAND() generates a pseudo-random decimal value.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-primary">

            <h2 class="fw-bold">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                SQL Server Numeric Functions are used to perform
                mathematical operations on numeric data. In this
                topic, we learned ABS(), CEILING(), FLOOR(), ROUND(),
                POWER(), SQRT(), SIGN() and RAND(). These functions
                are useful when working with prices, marks, quantities,
                calculations and other numerical values stored in
                database tables.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


